But your next level is:

Given a problem, why did you select this algorithm?

That's what NVIDIA/Google-style interviews can test.

For every problem, train yourself to answer:

1. What is the brute force solution?
2. What makes it inefficient?
3. What property can I exploit?
4. Which data structure helps?
5. Which algorithm fits that property?
6. Why is another algorithm worse?

For example:
          
          Problem
             ↓
          Identify structure
             ↓
          Is it sorted?
             ↓
          Binary Search
          
          Repeated lookup?
             ↓
          HashMap
          
          Contiguous range?
             ↓
          Sliding Window
          
          Explore all combinations?
             ↓
          Backtracking
          
          Optimal substructure?
             ↓
          Dynamic Programming
          
          Shortest path?
             ↓
          BFS / Dijkstra
          
          Dependencies?
             ↓
          Topological Sort
          
          Connectivity?
             ↓
         Union Find
===================================================================================================================================================
5. Build an "Algorithm Selection Framework"

I specifically want you to create your own mental cheat sheet.

For example:

| Problem characteristic   | Likely technique     |
| ------------------------ | -------------------- |
| Fast lookup              | HashMap/HashSet      |
| Sorted search            | Binary Search        |
| Two sorted sequences     | Two pointers         |
| Contiguous range         | Sliding Window       |
| Subarray sum             | Prefix Sum           |
| Top K                    | Heap                 |
| Dependencies             | Topological Sort     |
| Shortest unweighted path | BFS                  |
| Shortest weighted path   | Dijkstra             |
| All combinations         | Backtracking         |
| Overlapping subproblems  | DP                   |
| Connected components     | DFS/BFS/Union Find   |
| Range queries            | Segment Tree/Fenwick |
| Scheduling               | Heap/Greedy          |
| Interval overlap         | Sorting + Greedy     |
| Prefix matching          | Trie                 |




